\pard\sa200\f0\fs20\cf2\i [Diagramm \u8212? siehe HTML-Version f\u252?r die Illustration]\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs20\cf2\i [Diagramm \u8212? siehe HTML-Version f\u252?r die Illustration]\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Each addition changes the character of the rhythm. A single kick feels like a heartbeat. Two kicks feel like walking. The first snare adds a response \u8212? suddenly there is a conversation. The second snare completes the symmetry, and the pattern feels whole.\par
\pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b Recreating "Billie Jean" \u8212? Michael Jackson}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 One of the most famous drum patterns ever recorded belongs to Michael Jackson's "Billie Jean" (1982). The beat was played by session drummer Leon "Ndugu" Chancler, and its hypnotic, driving quality helped define the sound of an era. The pattern is almost identical to the basic beat, with one crucial difference: an extra kick drum hit on Step 11.\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 The pattern is as follows. Kick on steps 1, 9, and 11. Snare on steps 5 and 13.\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs20\cf2\i [Diagramm \u8212? siehe HTML-Version f\u252?r die Illustration]\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 That extra kick on Step 11 creates a sense of push \u8212? the beat leans forward into beat 4, building anticipation before the snare resolves the tension. This is called syncopation: placing a sound where you do not expect it. Step 11 falls between beats 3 and 4 \u8212? it is neither on a main beat nor on the expected backbeat. By arriving "early," it creates a subtle urgency that propels the groove forward. This is why "Billie Jean" feels so compelling: the kick drum is not merely keeping time, it is pulling you into the next moment.\par
\pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b Variationen zum Ausprobieren}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Once you have programmed the "Billie Jean" pattern, experiment with small changes. Each alteration shifts the feel of the beat in a distinct way:\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Move the kick on Step 11 to Step 10 instead. The syncopation arrives even earlier \u8212? the beat feels more urgent, more impatient. Then try Step 12: the push is gentler, almost lazy by comparison.\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Remove one snare hit. Keep only the snare on Step 5, or keep only the snare on Step 13. Notice how removing a single backbeat hit makes the pattern feel incomplete, lopsided \u8212? like a sentence missing its period.\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Add a kick on Step 5, right alongside the snare. When kick and snare land simultaneously, the combined hit is louder and more forceful. The beat becomes heavier, more aggressive. This technique is common in rock and metal drumming.\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 There is no wrong answer in these experiments. The goal is to develop your ear \u8212? to hear how each small change alters the character of the rhythm. Over time, you will begin to make these choices instinctively.\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Kick und Snare sind ein Gespr\u228?ch: tief und hoch, Boom und Crack, Fundament und Interpunktion. Jedes Pattern, das du von hier an machst, baut auf dieser Beziehung auf.\par
\pard\sb400\sa400\qc\f0\fs22 \emdash\emdash\emdash\par
\pard\sb600\sa200{\f0\fs36\b 3. Hi-Hats und Layering}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 GarageBand Beat Sequencer \u8212? Tutorial 3 von 7\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Du hast jetzt Kick und Snare \u8212? die zwei S\u228?ulen. Aber echte Beats haben ein drittes Element, das alles zusammenh\u228?lt: die Hi-Hat. Without it, even the best kick-and-snare pattern feels incomplete, like a sentence missing its connecting words.\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Die Hi-Hat liefert kontinuierliche Bewegung \u8212? a ticking pulse that gives the beat its drive and forward momentum. Where the kick and snare define the shape of a rhythm, the hi-hat fills it in, turning isolated hits into a living groove.\par
\pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b Die Rolle der Hi-Hat}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Ein heller, metallischer Tick-Klang \u8212? like a clock that never stops. While kick and snare mark specific moments, the hi-hat fills the space between them. It's what makes a beat feel like it's moving rather than just hitting.\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Think of it this way: the kick is your footstep, the snare is your hand clap, and the hi-hat is the constant rhythm of your breathing. You don't notice breathing until it stops \u8212? and you won't notice how much the hi-hat does until you remove it.\par
\pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b Drei Hi-Hat-Geschwindigkeiten}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 The hi-hat's power lies in how frequently it plays. The same kick and snare pattern can feel completely different depending on the hi-hat speed:\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Viertelnoten: hit on every beat (steps 1, 5, 9, 13) \u8212? slow, spacious\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Achtelnoten: hit every 2 steps (steps 1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13, 15) \u8212? standard rock/pop groove\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Sechzehntelnoten: hit on every Step (all 16) \u8212? fast, driving, energetic\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs20\cf2\i [Diagramm \u8212? siehe HTML-Version f\u252?r die Illustration]\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Each doubling makes the beat feel faster, even though the tempo stays the same.\par
\pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b Der komplette Drei-Schichten-Beat}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Now combine all three elements: kick (foundation), snare (backbeat), and hi-hat (motion). Each occupies its own row in the sequencer, its own role in the beat.\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs20\cf2\i [Diagramm \u8212? siehe HTML-Version f\u252?r die Illustration]\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Dies ist ein komplettes Drum-Pattern. Drei Kl\u228?nge, drei Rollen, die zusammenarbeiten.\par
\pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b Wie Layering funktioniert}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 A beat isn't assembled all at once \u8212? it's built in layers, each one adding a dimension the others lack. Here's how the same pattern develops as each element enters:\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs20\cf2\i [Diagramm \u8212? siehe HTML-Version f\u252?r die Illustration]\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs20\cf2\i [Diagramm \u8212? siehe HTML-Version f\u252?r die Illustration]\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs20\cf2\i [Diagramm \u8212? siehe HTML-Version f\u252?r die Illustration]\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Jede Schicht f\u252?gt eine Dimension hinzu. Das Ganze ist mehr als die Summe seiner Teile.\par
\pard\sb400\sa400\qc\f0\fs22 \emdash\emdash\emdash\par
\pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b Recreating "Stayin' Alive" \u8212? Bee Gees}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 A disco classic with driving hi-hats. The Bee Gees' 1977 hit is built on a relentless sixteenth-note hi-hat pattern \u8212? the signature of the disco era \u8212? paired with a syncopated kick drum that gives the groove its distinctive bounce.\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs20\cf2\i [Diagramm \u8212? siehe HTML-Version f\u252?r die Illustration]\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 The relentless hi-hat gives disco its urgency. The syncopated kick \u8212? hitting on steps 4 and 12, which fall between the main beats \u8212? gives it bounce. Notice how the kick avoids the downbeats on steps 5 and 13 where the snare lives, creating clear separation between the two.\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 This song's tempo (approximately 104 BPM) is famously used to teach CPR chest compression timing.\par
\pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b Zum Ausprobieren}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 With a three-layer beat in place, small changes produce large effects. Try these variations:\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Switch between sixteenth-note and eighth-note hi-hats on the same kick/snare pattern. Feel the difference in energy and density.\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Try removing hi-hats on the same steps as the kick. This creates "space" and makes the kick more prominent \u8212? a technique common in hip-hop.\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Try a hi-hat on every Step except the snare hits. How does that change the feel? The snare becomes more exposed, more dramatic.\par
\pard\sb400\sa400\qc\f0\fs22 \emdash\emdash\emdash\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Drei Instrumente, sechzehn Steps, unendliche M\u246?glichkeiten. From here, every pattern is a variation on this fundamental structure of foundation, punctuation, and motion.\par
\pard\sb400\sa400\qc\f0\fs22 \emdash\emdash\emdash\par
\pard\sb600\sa200{\f0\fs36\b 4. Velocity und Dynamik}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Bisher spielt jeder aktive Step mit der gleichen Lautst\u228?rke. Echte Schlagzeuger spielen nicht so. Some hits are loud, cutting through the mix. Others are barely audible, felt more than heard. The variation between them is what makes a rhythm feel alive.\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Velocity steuert, wie hart jeder Schlag klingt. Sie verwandelt ein mechanisches Pattern in etwas, das atmet und groovt.\par
\pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b Was ist Velocity}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Velocity ist die Intensit\u228?t jedes einzelnen Schlags, gemessen auf einer Skala von 0 (still) bis 127 (Maximum). The term comes from MIDI, where it originally referred to how fast a piano key was pressed \u8212? faster keystroke meant louder note.\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  H\u246?here Velocity = lauter, und oft heller oder sch\u228?rfer im Ton\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Niedrigere Velocity = leiser, und oft dumpfer oder runder im Ton\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 In GarageBand's Beat Sequencer, you switch to Velocity-Modus, then drag up or down on each Step to adjust its intensity. Each Step becomes individually controllable.\par
\pard\li720\sa200\f0\fs22\i It's the difference between tapping a table gently with your fingertip versus slapping it with your palm. Same surface, completely different sound.\par
\pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b Akzente \u8212? Wichtige Schl\u228?ge betonen}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 An accent is a deliberately louder hit. It tells the listener's ear where the rhythmic emphasis falls. Without accents, a pattern is a flat stream of equal pulses. With them, certain moments push forward and others recede, creating shape.\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 The most common accent pattern places louder hits on the main beats \u8212? steps 1, 5, 9, and 13 in a sixteen-Step grid \u8212? with softer hits in between. This is how most hi-hat patterns work in practice: a steady stream of notes, but the downbeats are louder.\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs20\cf2\i [Diagramm \u8212? siehe HTML-Version f\u252?r die Illustration]\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 The diagram uses opacity to represent velocity: fully opaque steps are loud accents, while faint steps are soft. All sixteen steps are active \u8212? the difference is entirely in how hard each one hits.\par
\pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b Ghost Notes \u8212? Die leise Textur}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Ghost Notes sind extrem leise Schl\u228?ge, typically at velocity 20\u8211?40 out of 127. They sit between the main hits, almost inaudible in isolation, but they fill space and add a sense of constant motion. Remove them and the pattern feels stiff. Add them back and it grooves.\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Ghost notes appear most commonly on the snare drum. The main snare hits land loud on beats 2 and 4 (steps 5 and 13). The ghost notes scatter quietly around them \u8212? not replacing the main hits, but inhabiting the gaps.\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs20\cf2\i [Diagramm \u8212? siehe HTML-Version f\u252?r die Illustration]\par
\pard\li720\sa200\f0\fs22\i Ghost notes are like background texture in a painting \u8212? you don't notice them consciously, but remove them and everything feels flat.\par
\pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b Ein komplettes Pattern mit Dynamik}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Here is a full three-row pattern with velocity applied throughout. The hi-hat has accents on the beats. The snare has loud main hits with ghost notes woven between. The kick hits at full velocity on its active steps. Together, they form a pattern with shape and weight.\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs20\cf2\i [Diagramm \u8212? siehe HTML-Version f\u252?r die Illustration]\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Compare this to a flat pattern where every hit is the same volume. The notes are identical \u8212? the feel is completely different.\par
\pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b Recreating "Superstition" \u8212? Stevie Wonder}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 One of the most dynamic drum grooves ever recorded. The drum part on "Superstition" (1972) is a masterclass in velocity control \u8212? every hit has a specific dynamic purpose. The ghost notes on the snare weave between the kick pattern, creating an interlocking groove that defines the song.\par
\pard\sb300\sa100{\f0\fs24\b Das Pattern}\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Hi-Hat: sixteenth notes (all 16 steps), accented on beats 1, 5, 9, 13\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Snare: main hits on steps 5, 13 (high velocity) + ghost notes on steps 3, 7, 10, 15 (very low velocity)\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Kick: steps 1, 7, 9, 11, 13 (syncopated funk pattern)\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs20\cf2\i [Diagramm \u8212? siehe HTML-Version f\u252?r die Illustration]\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 The ghost notes on the snare (steps 3, 7, 10, 15) weave between the kick pattern, creating an interlocking groove. Notice how the kick on Step 7 lines up with a snare ghost on Step 7 \u8212? both happening simultaneously, one loud and one barely there. This layering is funk: every sound has a specific dynamic purpose.\par
\pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b Velocity in GarageBand anwenden}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Step 1: Tap the Velocity button below the grid. The view changes to show velocity bars for each active step.\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Step 2: Drag UP on a Step to increase velocity (louder). Drag DOWN to decrease (softer).\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Step 3: Drag across multiple steps to set a gradient \u8212? useful for creating a swell or a fade.\par
\pard\sb300\sa100{\f0\fs24\b Praktischer Ansatz}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Start with all steps at medium velocity (around 80\u8211?90). Then raise the important beats \u8212? the accents \u8212? to full velocity (120\u8211?127). Finally, lower the in-between hits and ghost notes to 20\u8211?40. This three-layer approach (loud, medium, soft) gives you immediate dynamic shape without overthinking individual values.\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 You don't need mathematical precision. The ear responds to relative differences. As long as accents are noticeably louder and ghost notes are noticeably softer, the groove works.\par
\pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b Zum Ausprobieren}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Take any beat you built in the previous tutorials and apply what you've learned here:\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 1.  Accent the hi-hats. Set steps 1, 5, 9, and 13 to high velocity. Set the remaining steps to low velocity. Notice how the groove immediately develops a pulse, even though no notes were added or removed.\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 2.  Add ghost notes to the snare. Place 2\u8211?3 snare hits at very low velocity between the main backbeats. Does the pattern feel more alive? More complex?\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 3.  Weight the downbeat. Make the kick on Step 1 louder than the kick on Step 9. This gives the pattern a sense of "downbeat weight" \u8212? a clear beginning to each measure that the listener can anchor to.\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 4.  Experiment with the hi-hat gradient. Instead of just two levels (loud/soft), try making steps 1 and 9 loudest, steps 5 and 13 slightly less, and everything else soft. This creates a four-bar-feeling within a single measure.\par
\pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b Abschlie\u223?ender Gedanke}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Velocity ist das, was ein Pattern von einer Performance trennt. The same sequence of notes, shaped by dynamics, becomes music that breathes. A flat grid is a blueprint. A grid with velocity is a groove.\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 In the next tutorial, we'll look at how changing the number of steps and the playback rate lets you build patterns that feel like they move in different subdivisions \u8212? triplets, half-time, and beyond.\par
\pard\sb400\sa400\qc\f0\fs22 \emdash\emdash\emdash\par
\pard\sb600\sa200{\f0\fs36\b 5. Tempo und Genre}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Das gleiche Pattern bei verschiedenen Geschwindigkeiten gespielt wird zu v\u246?llig anderer Musik. Tempo \u8212? measured in BPM (beats per minute) \u8212? determines whether your beat feels like a slow walk or a sprint. Combined with specific pattern choices, tempo defines genre.\par
\pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b BPM erkl\u228?rt}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 BPM steht f\u252?r Beats per Minute: wie viele Beats in 60 Sekunden auftreten.\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  60 BPM \u8212? one beat per second. Very slow.\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  120 BPM \u8212? two beats per second. Moderate, walking pace.\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  140+ BPM \u8212? fast and energetic.\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 In GarageBand, tap the tempo display in the control bar to change it. You can type a number directly or drag to adjust.\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 A 16-Step bar at 120 BPM takes exactly 2 seconds to play through. At 60 BPM, the same 16 steps take 4 seconds. The pattern is identical \u8212? only the speed changes.\par
\pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b Genre-\u220?bersicht}\par
\pard\sa100\f0\fs20\b Genre  |  Typical BPM  |  Kick Character  |  Hi-Hat Style\par
\pard\sa50\f0\fs20 Hip-Hop  |  70\u8211?100  |  Sparse, heavy  |  Fast sixteenths or sparse\par
\pard\sa50\f0\fs20 Pop / Rock  |  100\u8211?130  |  Beats 1 & 3  |  Eighth notes\par
\pard\sa50\f0\fs20 House / EDM  |  120\u8211?130  |  Every beat (four-on-the-floor)  |  Offbeats\par
\pard\sa50\f0\fs20 Drum & Bass  |  160\u8211?180  |  Syncopated, fast  |  Complex patterns\par
\par
\pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b Pattern: Hip-Hop (70\u8211?90 BPM)}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Characteristics: slow, sparse, punchy. The space between hits feels wide because of the low tempo. The kick is syncopated and heavy.\par
\pard\sb300\sa100{\f0\fs24\b Pattern}\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Hi-Hat: all 16 steps (sixteenth notes \u8212? rapid at this tempo, creating the trap feel)\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Snare/Clap: steps 5, 13\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Kick: steps 1, 4, 10, 13 (sparse, syncopated)\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs20\cf2\i [Diagramm \u8212? siehe HTML-Version f\u252?r die Illustration]\par
\pard\li720\sa200\f0\fs22\i At 75 BPM, the sixteenth-note hi-hats feel rapid despite the slow tempo. This contrast between slow kick and fast hi-hat defines modern trap and hip-hop.\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 GarageBand kit suggestion: "Trap Door" or "808"\par
\pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b Pattern: House/EDM (125 BPM)}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Characteristics: four-on-the-floor kick (the kick fires on every beat), offbeat hi-hats, steady and driving. The pattern is simple but the relentless pulse creates momentum.\par
\pard\sb300\sa100{\f0\fs24\b Pattern}\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Hi-Hat: steps 3, 7, 11, 15 (offbeats only \u8212? between the kicks)\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Snare/Clap: steps 5, 13\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Kick: steps 1, 5, 9, 13 (every beat \u8212? four-on-the-floor)\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs20\cf2\i [Diagramm \u8212? siehe HTML-Version f\u252?r die Illustration]\par
\pard\li720\sa200\f0\fs22\i Four-on-the-Floor bedeutet, dass die Kick auf jedem einzelnen Beat feuert. The hi-hats play only between kicks, creating a push-pull that makes people dance.\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 GarageBand kit suggestion: "Neon" or "Hacienda"\par
\pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b Die beiden im Vergleich}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Place both patterns together to see how tempo and placement create completely different feelings from the same grid.\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs20\cf2\i [Diagramm \u8212? siehe HTML-Version f\u252?r die Illustration]\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs20\cf2\i [Diagramm \u8212? siehe HTML-Version f\u252?r die Illustration]\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Same 16-Step grid, same three instruments, completely different music. The combination of tempo and pattern placement defines the genre.\par
\pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b Das gleiche Pattern bei verschiedenen Tempi}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Take the basic beat from earlier tutorials: kick on steps 1 and 9, snare on steps 5 and 13, hi-hat on every odd Step (eighth notes).\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  At 70 BPM: feels like a slow hip-hop groove\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  At 120 BPM: feels like a standard rock beat\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  At 160 BPM: feels like punk or drum & bass territory\par
\pard\li720\sa200\f0\fs22\i You don't need to learn new patterns to explore new genres. Sometimes, just changing the tempo transforms everything.\par
\pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b Kit-Auswahl}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 The drum sounds you choose reinforce the genre. The pattern and tempo establish the rhythm, but the timbres tell the listener what world they're in.\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Acoustic kits: rock, pop, jazz\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  808 / Trap kits: hip-hop, trap, lo-fi\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Electronic kits: house, techno, EDM\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 In GarageBand, tap the kit name at the bottom-left of the Beat Sequencer to browse and change kits. Each kit contains the same instruments (kick, snare, hi-hat, etc.) but with different sounds.\par
\pard\li720\sa200\f0\fs22\i Try playing your hip-hop pattern with an acoustic rock kit. It won't sound "wrong" \u8212? but it won't feel like hip-hop. Sound design is the final ingredient in genre identity.\par
\pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b Zum Ausprobieren}\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 1.  Create the house pattern, then slowly decrease the tempo from 125 to 80. At what point does it stop feeling like house music?\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 2.  Swap between hip-hop and house kits on the same pattern. Notice how sound design shapes genre identity even when the rhythm stays the same.\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 3.  Try four-on-the-floor kick at 160 BPM \u8212? this enters techno territory.\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 4.  Program the hip-hop pattern and increase tempo to 140. Does it become something new?\par
\pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b Abschluss}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Genre ist keine starre Schublade. Es ist eine Kombination aus Tempo, Pattern und Klang. By adjusting these three variables on the same 16-Step grid, you can move between any style. The grid doesn't change \u8212? only what you put in it and how fast it plays.\par
\pard\sb400\sa400\qc\f0\fs22 \emdash\emdash\emdash\par
\pard\sb600\sa200{\f0\fs36\b 6. Songstruktur und Variation}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 GarageBand Beat Sequencer \u8212? Tutorial 6 von 7\par
\pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b Einleitung}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Ein einzelnes sich wiederholendes Pattern ist ein Loop. Ein Song besteht aus mehreren Patterns, die in Abschnitte arrangiert sind und eine Reise erzeugen \u8212? tension, release, surprise, resolution.\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 This tutorial covers how to vary your patterns across sections and arrange them into a complete piece. The grid stays the same; what changes is how much of it you fill and when.\par
\pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b Song-Abschnitte}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Die meiste popul\u228?re Musik folgt einer bekannten Struktur. Each section serves a purpose:\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Intro \u8212? establishes the mood, often starts sparse\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Strophe \u8212? the main body, moderate energy, leaves room for melody or vocals\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Refrain \u8212? the peak, maximum energy, fullest arrangement\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Bridge \u8212? contrast, something different to create surprise before returning to the chorus\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Outro \u8212? winds down, often mirrors the intro\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 A typical order:\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Intro \u8594? Verse \u8594? Chorus \u8594? Verse \u8594? Chorus \u8594? Bridge \u8594? Chorus \u8594? Outro\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Not every song follows this exactly, but the principle holds: sections alternate between lower and higher energy, creating movement over time.\par
\pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b Wie sich Drum-Patterns zwischen Abschnitten \u228?ndern}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 The beat should support the energy level of each section. Here is how a typical drum arrangement varies:\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Intro: minimal \u8212? maybe just hi-hat or a simple kick pattern\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Verse: basic beat, moderate density\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Chorus: full kit, more hits, possibly faster hi-hats or added percussion\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Bridge: stripped back or a completely different pattern\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 The following four grids show the same instruments across four sections. Notice how density alone creates entirely different energy levels:\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Each section uses the same grid and same instruments \u8212? but different densities create different energy.\par
\pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b Variationstechniken}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 You do not need entirely new patterns for each section. Small, deliberate changes are enough:\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Adding: introduce a new sound \u8212? layer a clap on the snare, bring in a shaker or tambourine\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Subtracting: remove an element \u8212? drop the kick for 4 bars, pull back the hi-hats\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Density: switch hi-hats from eighth notes to sixteenths for more energy, or from sixteenths to quarters for less\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Syncopation: move kicks off the main beats for a different feel without changing the number of hits\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 The simplest variation: take your chorus pattern and remove one layer. That's your verse.\par
\pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b Der Aufbau}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 A build-up creates anticipation before a section change, usually leading into the chorus. The technique: gradually add elements or increase density over 2 to 4 bars.\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Example progression:\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Bar 1: hi-hat only\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Bar 2: hi-hat + kick\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Bar 3: hi-hat + kick + snare\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Bar 4: full pattern (the chorus drops)\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Each bar adds one layer. By bar 4, all elements are present and the listener feels the arrival of the chorus as a release of built-up anticipation.\par
\pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b Recreating "Uptown Funk" \u8212? Bruno Mars}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 This song has distinct drum energy across its sections. The verse stays relatively restrained; the chorus explodes with density.\par
\pard\sb300\sa100{\f0\fs24\b Strophen-Pattern}\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Kick: steps 1, 7, 9, 11\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Snare: steps 5, 13\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Hi-Hat: eighth notes (steps 1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13, 15)\par
\pard\sb300\sa100{\f0\fs24\b Refrain-Pattern}\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Kick: steps 1, 4, 7, 9, 11, 13\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Snare: steps 5, 13\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Hi-Hat: all 16 steps (sixteenth notes)\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Extra clap: steps 5, 13 (layered with snare for extra punch)\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Notice: same song, same instruments, but the chorus has more kick hits and faster hi-hats. Small changes, big energy difference.\par
\pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b Patterns in GarageBand aufnehmen}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Once you have created a pattern in the Beat Sequencer, you need to record it to the timeline to build a song:\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 1.  Create your pattern in the Beat Sequencer.\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 2.  Tap the Record button in the control bar.\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 3.  The pattern records as a loop to the track \u8212? let it run for as many bars as you need for that section.\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 4.  Stop recording.\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 5.  Modify the pattern in the Beat Sequencer (add or remove hits for the next section).\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 6.  Record again \u8212? a new region appears on the timeline.\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 7.  In Tracks View, you will see colored regions. Each is a recorded pattern section.\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 8.  Arrange these regions to build your song: copy, move, and duplicate sections as needed.\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 This is how a single Beat Sequencer becomes a full arrangement \u8212? one pattern at a time, recorded and placed in sequence.\par
\pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b Eine einfache Songstruktur-\u220?bung}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Create three variations of the same basic beat:\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Pattern A (sparse \u8212? for intro and verse): kick and hi-hat only, eighth-note hi-hats\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Pattern B (full \u8212? for chorus): kick, snare, hi-hat at sixteenths, plus a percussion layer\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Pattern C (breakdown \u8212? for bridge): just snare and sparse kick, no hi-hat\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Record each as a separate section on the timeline. Then arrange them in this order:\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 A \u8594? B \u8594? A \u8594? B \u8594? C \u8594? B\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 You have just composed a song structure using only pattern variation.\par
\pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b Zum Ausprobieren}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Listen to any song you like. Can you identify where the drums get fuller (likely the chorus) and where they get simpler (likely the verse)? Most songs make this shift obvious once you listen for it.\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Try creating a 4-bar build-up that leads into your fullest pattern. Record the build-up bars individually, each slightly denser than the last, then let the full chorus pattern drop on bar 5.\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Experiment with removing the kick entirely for 4 bars, then bringing it back. This "drop" technique is powerful \u8212? the absence of the lowest frequency makes the listener lean forward, and its return provides physical satisfaction.\par
\pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b Abschluss}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Ein Song ist eine Abfolge von Entscheidungen dar\u252?ber, was hinzugef\u252?gt und was entfernt wird. Das Raster gibt dir das Vokabular; das Arrangement gibt dir die Erz\u228?hlung.\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 You do not need dozens of instruments or complex harmonies to create a compelling piece. A single drum kit, varied thoughtfully across sections, already tells a story of tension and release. The next tutorial covers how to refine these ideas into polished, finished work.\par
\pard\sb400\sa400\qc\f0\fs22 \emdash\emdash\emdash\par
\pard\sb600\sa200{\f0\fs36\b 7. Erweiterte Funktionen und Komposition}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Du hast jetzt die grundlegenden F\u228?higkeiten: Kl\u228?nge auf einem Raster platzieren, Instrumente schichten, Dynamik mit Velocity formen, Tempo und Genre w\u228?hlen und Songstruktur durch Abschnitte und \u220?berg\u228?nge aufbauen.\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 This final tutorial introduces GarageBand's remaining Beat Sequencer features \u8212? Note Repeat, Chance, and Loop Start/End \u8212? and guides you through composing an original piece using everything you have learned.\par
\pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b Note Repeat}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Note Repeat unterteilt einen einzelnen Step in schnelle Wiederholungen \u8212? creating drum rolls, hi-hat flutters, and fills. One Step that normally plays once can be split into 2, 3, 4, 6, or 8 rapid hits within the same time span.\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 In GarageBand: tap Note Repeat mode in the toolbar, then drag UP on a Step to increase the number of repetitions.\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Each row represents the same single step. The time span is identical \u8212? only the number of hits within it changes.\par
\pard\sb300\sa100{\f0\fs24\b Typische Verwendungen}\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Snare roll on the last 1\u8211?2 steps of a bar \u8212? creates a fill before the next section\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Hi-hat flutter on specific steps for rhythmic interest\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Building tension before a chorus drop\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Steps 15\u8211?16 each contain four rapid snare hits, producing a tight roll that propels the listener into the next bar.\par
\pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b Chance}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Chance f\u252?hrt Wahrscheinlichkeit ein \u8212? a Step with reduced Chance may or may not play each time the pattern loops. This creates subtle, ever-changing variation without manually editing the pattern.\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Each Step has a probability from 0% (never plays) to 100% (always plays).\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 In GarageBand: tap Chance mode, then drag DOWN on a Step to reduce its probability.\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Steps at 50% (shown lighter) play only half the time. Each loop sounds slightly different \u8212? organic and unpredictable.\par
\pard\sb300\sa100{\f0\fs24\b Beste Verwendungen}\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Hi-hat with some steps at 50% \u8212? creates organic variation between loops\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Ghost notes with low probability (30%) \u8212? unpredictable texture\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Extra percussion hits at 40\u8211?60% \u8212? occasional surprises\par
\pard\li720\sa200\f0\fs22\i Chance mimics what real drummers do naturally \u8212? small, unconscious variations that make repetition feel alive rather than robotic.\par
\pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b Loop Start/End}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Jede Reihe kann ihre eigene unabh\u228?ngige Loop-L\u228?nge haben. By default all rows loop through all 16 steps. But you can set one row to loop through only 12 steps, or 7 steps, or any other length.\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 When rows have different lengths, they create polyrhythm \u8212? patterns that phase against each other and only realign after many repetitions.\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 In GarageBand: tap Loop Start/End mode, then drag the edges of a row to shorten or lengthen it.\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Example: Set the hi-hat row to 14 steps while kick and snare remain at 16. The hi-hat pattern will gradually shift relative to the other instruments, creating evolving rhythmic relationships.\par
\pard\li720\sa200\f0\fs22\i Polyrhythm is an advanced technique used extensively in African music, electronic music, and experimental composition.\par
\pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b Deinen eigenen Beat komponieren \u8212? Ein Prozess}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 With all tools now available to you, here is a structured approach to building a beat from nothing:\par
\pard\li720\sa200\f0\fs22\i This is the same process professional producers follow. The grid is the same whether you are a beginner or a chart-topping artist.\par
\pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b Referenz: Alle Beat-Sequencer-Steuerungen}\par
\pard\sa100\f0\fs20\b Mode  |  Function  |  Gesture\par
\pard\sa50\f0\fs20 Step On/Off  |  Activate or deactivate steps  |  Tap\par
\pard\sa50\f0\fs20 Velocity  |  Adjust hit volume per step  |  Drag up/down\par
\pard\sa50\f0\fs20 Note Repeat  |  Subdivide a Step into rolls  |  Drag up for more repeats\par
\pard\sa50\f0\fs20 Chance  |  Set play probability  |  Drag down to reduce\par
\pard\sa50\f0\fs20 Loop Start/End  |  Set row loop boundaries  |  Drag left/right edges\par
\par
\pard\sb300\sa100{\f0\fs24\b Weitere Steuerungen}\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Patterns button: save, load, or create new patterns\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Kit name: change drum kit sounds\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Inspector: pattern length, swing, Step resolution\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Dice: generate a random pattern\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Record: capture pattern to the arrangement track\par
\pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b Swing}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Swing shifts the timing of off-beat steps slightly late, creating a "shuffle" feel. Rather than all steps being evenly spaced, the off-beats arrive a fraction later than expected.\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 In the Inspector settings, adjust the swing amount:\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  0% swing = perfectly straight, mechanical timing\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  ~30% swing = relaxed, human feel (hip-hop, lo-fi)\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  50%+ swing = pronounced shuffle (jazz, swing, some house music)\par
\pard\li720\sa200\f0\fs22\i Swing is subtle but powerful. A hip-hop beat with 30% swing feels relaxed and human. The same beat at 0% swing feels rigid.\par
\pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b Eine vollst\u228?ndige Kompositions\u252?bung}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Compose a piece with at least three sections:\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Section A (Verse): A pattern with moderate density and a clear groove. Use the kick and snare as your anchors. Keep the hi-hat consistent but not overly busy. Apply velocity for natural dynamics.\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Section B (Chorus): A fuller pattern with more energy. Consider adding more kick hits, denser hi-hats, or additional percussion layers. Raise the overall velocity slightly to create a sense of lift.\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Transition: On the last bar of Section A, apply Note Repeat (\u215?4 or \u215?8) to the snare on steps 15\u8211?16. This creates a roll that propels the listener into the chorus.\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Additional considerations:\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Apply velocity variation throughout \u8212? no flat dynamics\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Add swing if the genre calls for it\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Choose an appropriate drum kit for your genre\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Use Chance on one or two secondary percussion elements\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Record each section and arrange them in Tracks View: A\u8211?A\u8211?B\u8211?B or A\u8211?B\u8211?A\u8211?B\par
\pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b Wie es weitergeht}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 The Beat Sequencer is one instrument within GarageBand. Once your rhythmic foundation is solid, consider these next steps:\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Add a bass line using Smart Bass \u8212? it naturally complements the kick drum pattern\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Layer melodic elements using the keyboard or Smart Piano\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Explore Apple Loops to add pre-made musical phrases alongside your beats\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Record vocals or live instruments over your rhythmic arrangements\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Export and share your compositions as audio files\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Der Step Sequencer ist ein vollst\u228?ndiges kompositorisches Instrument. Sechzehn Steps, eine Handvoll Kl\u228?nge und die Prinzipien von Rhythmus, Dynamik und Struktur \u8212? das sind die gleichen Werkzeuge, die jeden Beat gebaut haben, den du je geh\u246?rt hast. Die einzige Variable jetzt sind deine kreativen Entscheidungen.\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 GarageBand Beat Sequencer Tutorials \u8212? 7 von 7\par
